{0 Error handling}

Parseff exposes four main APIs for error handling:

- {!val:Parseff.fail}: fail with a string message
- {!val:Parseff.error}: fail with a typed custom value
- {!val:Parseff.expect}: relabel failures from another parser
- {!val:Parseff.context} and {!val:Parseff.cut}: name a construct and decide it

{1 [fail]}

Abort parsing with a message. The message is returned as
[Error { kind = Failure msg; ... }].

{@ocaml skip[val fail : ?from:int -> ?notes:Error.note list -> string -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
let byte () =
  let n =
    int_of_string
      (Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1
         (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9')
         ~label:"digit")
  in
  if n >= 0 && n <= 255 then n
  else Parseff.fail "number must be between 0 and 255"

let () =
  match Parseff.parse "300" byte with
  | Error { kind = Failure msg; span; _ } ->
      (* span.start = 3, msg = "number must be between 0 and 255" *)
      Printf.printf "Error at %d: %s\n" span.start msg
  | Ok n -> Printf.printf "Got: %d\n" n
  | Error _ -> ()
]}

Use this when a human-readable string is enough.

[~from] makes the error cover the bytes from [from] to the current position
instead of a single position, so the rendered error underlines the whole value.
[~notes] attaches annotations: a note with a range is underlined in the source
excerpt with its text, a note without one is printed as a [* note:] line.

{@ocaml[
let octet () =
  let start = Parseff.position () in
  let n =
    int_of_string
      (Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9')
         ~label:"digit")
  in
  if n <= 255 then n
  else
    Parseff.fail ~from:start
      ~notes:[ { Parseff.Error.at = None; text = "octets go from 0 to 255" } ]
      "octet out of range"

let () =
  match Parseff.parse "10.300" (fun () ->
      let a = octet () in
      let _ = Parseff.char '.' in
      (a, octet ()))
  with
  | Error e -> print_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"address" e)
  | Ok _ -> ()
]}

which prints:

{v
address:1:4: error: octet out of range
1 | 10.300
  |    ^^^
  * note: octets go from 0 to 255
v}

{1 [error]}

Abort parsing with a custom error value. {!type:Parseff.Error.user} is an
extensible variant: declare your own cases and match them in [kind].

{@ocaml skip[val error : ?from:int -> ?notes:Error.note list -> Error.user -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
type Parseff.Error.user += Too_large of int | Negative of int

let number () =
  let s =
    Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') ~label:"digit"
  in
  let n = int_of_string s in
  if n > 255 then Parseff.error (Too_large n)
  else if n < 0 then Parseff.error (Negative n)
  else n

let () =
  match Parseff.parse "300" number with
  | Error { kind = User (Too_large n); span; _ } ->
      Printf.printf "Number %d too large at position %d\n" n span.start
  | Error { kind = User (Negative n); span; _ } ->
      Printf.printf "Negative number %d at position %d\n" n span.start
  | Error _ -> Printf.printf "Other error\n"
  | Ok n -> Printf.printf "Got %d\n" n
]}

Use this when callers need to pattern match on specific failure cases. [~from]
and [~notes] work as in [fail].

A typed error behaves like any other failure for backtracking: {!val:Parseff.or_}
and the repetition combinators absorb it and try something else. If the parse
then fails and the typed error is at the furthest failure position, it is the
error that [parse] returns. At the same position a typed error wins over an
[Expected] error, and the first of two typed errors wins.

A case without a payload works too:

{@ocaml[
type Parseff.Error.user += Too_large_quick | Negative_quick

let number_quick () =
  let s =
    Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') ~label:"digit"
  in
  let n = int_of_string s in
  if n > 255 then Parseff.error Too_large_quick
  else if n < 0 then Parseff.error Negative_quick
  else n
]}

{1 [expect]}

Run a parser and replace its failure message with a clearer description. The
error reads [expected <description>].

{@ocaml skip[val expect : string -> (unit -> 'a) -> 'a]}

{@ocaml[
let dot () =
  Parseff.expect "a dot separator" (fun () -> Parseff.char '.')

let number () =
  let s =
    Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') ~label:"digit"
  in
  int_of_string s

let ip_address () =
  let a = number () in
  let _ = dot () in
  let b = number () in
  let _ = dot () in
  let c = number () in
  let _ = dot () in
  let d = number () in
  Parseff.end_of_input ();
  (a, b, c, d)
]}

Without [expect], a failed [char '.'] reports [expected '.']. With [expect], it
reports [expected a dot separator].

[expect] replaces only what the parser expected at the position where it
started. If the parser got further before it failed, its own message stays,
because it is more precise. See {!page:combinators}.

{1 [context] and [cut]}

{!val:Parseff.context} names a construct, for example ["array"]. A failure
inside it remembers the constructs that were open, outermost first.
{!val:Parseff.cut} says that the construct is decided: after [cut], a failure
inside the construct is not retried by an enclosing [or_], [many] or [sep_by].
Use [cut] after the token that identifies the construct, such as [let] in a
[let] statement. See {!page:combinators}.

{1 Which error a parse reports}

When a parse fails, Parseff reports the {e furthest} failure: the failure of any
primitive that happened at the highest input position, also inside branches that
[or_], [many], [sep_by], [optional] or [fold_left] abandoned. Everything that was
expected at that position is merged into one message, sorted and without
duplicates:

{@ocaml skip[
(* "x" with one_of [consume "if"; consume "else"; consume "while"] *)
(* expected "else", "if", or "while", found 'x' *)
]}

This also explains errors after a loop. With
[sep_by (fun () -> consume "ab") (fun () -> char ',')] followed by
[end_of_input], the input ["ab,ab,ax"] reports [expected "b"] at offset 7. The
loop stopped because ["ax"] is not ["ab"], and that is further than the
[end_of_input] failure at offset 5.

A failure at the end of the input keeps what was expected. On ["[1, 2"] the
error says [expected ',' or ']', found end of input].

[consume] fails at the first byte that differs from the literal and expects the
rest of it. {!val:Parseff.commit} forgets the failures at or beyond its position,
so a committed branch reports only what it expected itself.

{1 The error value}

A failed parse returns [Error e], where [e] is a {!type:Parseff.Error.t}:

{@ocaml skip[
type Error.t = {
  kind : Error.kind;
  span : Error.range;  (** the bytes the error is about, empty at the end of the input *)
  position : Error.position option;
      (** line and column of [span.start], [None] when the line is not known *)
  frames : Error.frame list;  (** open constructs, outermost first *)
  notes : Error.note list;
  excerpt : Error.excerpt;
}
]}

[Error.t] and the types it holds are [private]: you can read and match them,
not build them. {!type:Parseff.Error.note} and {!type:Parseff.Error.range} stay
buildable, for [~notes] of {!val:Parseff.fail}.

[kind] says what happened:

- [Expected { items; found; more }]: a primitive failed. [items] is everything that
  was expected at the furthest failure position, sorted and without duplicates:
  [Char], [Literal], [Label] or [End_of_input]. [found] is [Text] with the
  text at that position, [End] at the end of the input, [Short n] when a
  read of a fixed number of bytes, such as [take], found only [n] bytes before
  the end of the input, or [Value] with the integer read in the same width when
  [BE.int16] and its relatives do not match. Characters print as ['x'],
  literals as ["abc"] and labels as written. [items] holds at most 64 items;
  [more] counts the alternatives past that, [0] otherwise.
- [Failure msg]: a failure raised with {!val:Parseff.fail}, or ["no alternative
  matched"] when [one_of []] is the only failure at the furthest position.
  Failures of primitives are always [Expected], so they merge with the other
  alternatives that were expected at the same position. Unlike [Expected],
  [fail] is not caught by backtracking combinators ({!val:Parseff.or_},
  {!val:Parseff.many}, etc.) or relabeling combinators
  ({!val:Parseff.expect}, {!val:Parseff.one_of_labeled}).
- [User e]: a value given to {!val:Parseff.error}.
- [Depth_limit_exceeded { limit }]: a {!val:Parseff.rec_} call exceeded the
  [~max_depth] limit.
- [Backtrack_window_exceeded { limit; oldest }]: a streaming parser needed more rewind
  history than [~backtrack_window] allows.

The match on [kind] is exhaustive: the compiler tells you when a case is
missing. [User] holds an {!type:Parseff.Error.user}, which is extensible, so
match it with a final [_] case.

{@ocaml skip[
match Parseff.parse input parser with
| Ok value -> value
| Error { kind = User (Too_large n); _ } -> handle_too_large n
| Error e -> prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"input" e); exit 1
]}

{1 Rendering errors}

{!val:Parseff.render_error} prints an error with its source line, a caret under
the failure and what the parser was doing:

{v
input.json:4:5: error: expected ',' or '}', found '"'
2 |     "config": {
  | ╭─────────────╯
3 | │     "a": 1
4 | │     "b": 2
  | ╰─────^
  * while parsing object, from 2:13
  * while parsing object, from 1:1
v}

- The header is [name:line:col: error: message]. Without [~name] it starts at
  [line:col].
- The message is [expected A, B, or C, found X]. A message lists at most 8
  expected items, then [, or N more alternatives]. [found X] is a quoted
  identifier-like run (up to 32 bytes, [found 'abc'...] when it is cut) or one
  code point, with control bytes, non-breaking spaces and other invisible code
  points escaped as [\x1B] or [\u{00A0}], or [end of input]. A [take] that
  finds too few bytes says [found only 3 (end of input)], and a binary integer
  that differs says [expected int16be 0x0001, found 0x0002].
- When the line of the error is not known, which happens in streaming when the
  position is older than the buffered input, the header is
  [input.json: byte N: error: message] and there is no excerpt. The [*] lines
  are then indented by two spaces.
- Carets cover the span, one per code point. Tabs in the line are kept, so the
  caret lines up for any tab width. Control bytes, invalid UTF-8 bytes and C1
  control characters in the line print as the escapes of the message, such as
  [\x1B], and the carets cover the whole escape.
- [* while parsing name, from line:col] lines come from {!val:Parseff.context},
  innermost first, at most five; when only one more is hidden it is printed
  too. A construct made with [~delimited:true] that started on an earlier line
  is shown as a bar that joins its opening token to the caret: [╭─╯] under the
  opener, [│] on the lines in between and [╰──^] at the failure. A single
  hidden line between the opener and the failure is shown, longer gaps become
  a [...] row in the gutter.
- Notes print as [* note:] lines, or as a [-] underline on their line when they
  have a range.
- A {!val:Parseff.rec_} nested deeper than [~max_depth] and a streaming parse
  that exceeds [~backtrack_window] keep the [* while parsing] lines that were
  open, and end with [* help: raise ~max_depth] or
  [* help: call commit or cut earlier, or raise ~backtrack_window].
- [~user] is a printer [Error.user -> string] for [User] errors. Without it, a user error prints [user error (no ~user printer given)].
- No row has trailing spaces.

[~source] gives the input to read the lines from. Without it the lines kept
inside the error are used: the error line, the line before it and the line of
the opener, each cut to 240 bytes around the failure. That is enough for
in-memory and streaming parses; with a streaming source the old lines may be gone
and the frame line then says [from byte N].
[Parseff.Error.message] returns only the one-line message.

[~columns] chooses the unit of the column in the header: bytes by default, or
code points with [`Code_points] (see {!page:utf8}), also in the columns of the
[* while parsing] lines. Carets always count code points.

{v
input.json:1:7: error: expected ',' or ']', found '"'   (bytes)
input.json:1:6: error: expected ',' or ']', found '"'   (code points)
1 | ["é" "b"]
  |      ^
v}

{1 Migrating from 0.4}

[Error { pos; error }] with built-in polymorphic variants became [Error e] with
a record. The common migration is one line:

{@ocaml skip[
(* before *)
| Error { pos; error = `Expected msg } -> Printf.printf "%d: %s\n" pos msg
(* after *)
| Error e -> prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error ~name:"input" e)
]}

User errors move from the variant row into [kind]:
[Error { error = `Too_large n; _ }] becomes
[Error { kind = User (Too_large n); _ }], with [type Parseff.Error.user += Too_large of int]. [`Failure msg] becomes [Failure msg].
The 0.4 messages of primitives that were not expectations (invalid UTF-8, failed
regex, mismatched [BE.int16]) are now [Expected] items: [valid UTF-8],
[text matching the pattern] and [int16be 0xFFFE].
[`Unexpected_end_of_input] is an [Expected] error whose [found] is [End], so it
now says what was expected. The position is [e.span.start].
